{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018C7P9hLu5jCEKSqyaKtLsZ","type":"tool_result","content":"\\leanentry{reinsert}{The old move needs its reinsertion test (path on five vertices)} \\leannames{reinsert_needs_test} \\\\\n\\leanentry{memo}{Solvability of a state does not depend on the path to it (the memo)} \\leannames{solvable_iff_of_cl_eq} \\\\\n\\leanentry{fakeold}{A node with an unrefuted old move can record a solvable state} \\leannames{exec_fake_oldMove} \\\\\n\\leanentry{nodesound}{Node-soundness of a filter (Section~\\ref{sec:theorem})} \\leannames{NodeSoundAt} \\\\\n\\leanentry{main}{Theorem~\\ref{thm:main}, our corrected search is sound} \\leannames{exec_repairedFullFilter_sound, exec_repairedFullFilter_pathwidth, exec_repairedFullFilter_mospValue} \\\\\n\\leanentry{notsound}{Under the previous algorithm the filter is not node-sound} \\leannames{not_codeFilterSound_cexGraph} \\\\\n\\leanentry{wrong}{Figure~\\ref{fig:wrong}: the previous algorithm refutes 6 and finds 7 on the 34-customer instance, whose optimum is 6 and pathwidth 5; its run is a run of the search with the published filter, which is therefore not sound there} \\leannames{previousAlgorithm_wrong_optimum, not_filterSound_codeFilter} \\\\\n\\leanentry{minor}{Theorem~\\ref{thm:minor}: contracting an edge (merging $v$ into $u$, as the solvers do) or deleting a vertex or an edge never increases pathwidth; along a legal contraction sequence, a minor of pathwidth above $w$ forces pathwidth above $w$, and on the MOSP graph more than $w+1$ open stacks} \\leannames{pathwidth_contractEdge_le, pathwidth_induce_le, pathwidth_mono, pathwidth_minorOf_le, pathwidth_gt_of_minorOf, MOSPInstance.lt_mospValue_of_minorOf} \\\\\n\\leanentry{grid}{The $(h,w)$-grid has pathwidth $\\min(h,w)$, except the $1\\times1$ grid (pathwidth $0$); \\citet[Theorem~4.1]{EllisWarren2008}. Upper bound by the row-major layout's bandwidth; lower bound directly on vertex separation, at the first prefix that completes a row or a column} \\leannames{pathwidth_grid, pathwidth_grid_le, min_le_pathwidth_grid, pathwidth_grid_one_one, pathwidth_grid_square} \\\\\n\\leanentry{hypercube}{The $d$-dimensional hypercube has pathwidth $\\sum_{i<d}\\binom{i}{\\lfloor i/2\\rfloor}$ \\citep[Theorem~2]{ChandranKavitha2006}, with Harper's vertex-isoperimetric theorem \\citep{Harper1966} proved by compression. Upper bound by the layout along Harper's simplicial order, lower bound at a prefix of every size, both by induction on $d$ and on vertex separation} \\leannames{pathwidth_hypercube, pathwidth_cubeGraph, Hypercube.harper, Hypercube.card_nbhd_init_le, Hypercube.exists_init_profile} \\\\\n\\leanentry{mycielski}{For every graph $G$, its Mycielskian $\\mu(G)$ (originals, a shadow of each vertex adjacent to its neighbours, and an apex adjacent to the shadows) has $\\mathrm{pw}(G)+1\\le\\mathrm{pw}(\\mu(G))\\le 2\\,\\mathrm{pw}(G)+1$. The upper bound interleaves each vertex with its shadow and places the apex last; we found no published statement of it. The lower bound keeps the bags meeting the apex and the shadows} \\leannames{pathwidth_mycielskian_le, pathwidth_add_one_le_mycielskian} \\\\\n\\leanentry{emptypair}{Step~1 of Appendix~\\ref{app:random}: at every position of every layout the prefix and the inactive suffix are an empty pair, so for every $j\\le n$ some $j$-set has closed neighbourhood of size at most $j+\\pw$ (Harper's bound in layout form), and $n-2s^{*}-2\\le\\pw$ for the empty-pair index $s^{*}$} \\leannames{exists_empty_pair_of_layout, exists_closedNbhd_card_le, exists_empty_pair, card_sub_two_mul_emptyPairIndex_sub_two_le_pathwidth} \\\\\n\\leanentry{firstmoment}{Step~2 of Appendix~\\ref{app:random}: the number of graphs on $n$ labelled vertices of pathwidth at most $k$ is at most $\\binom nt\\binom{n-t}{t-1}2^{\\binom n2-t(t-1)}$, $t=\\lceil(n-k)/2\\rceil$, and the $G(n,p)$-weighted sum of those graphs is at most $\\binom nt\\binom{n-t}{t-1}(1-p)^{t(t-1)}$; there are $2^{\\binom n2}$ graphs on $n$ labelled vertices} \\leannames{card_pathwidth_le_le, weightedSum_graphsOfPathwidthLe_le, card_emptyPairGraphs, card_simpleGraph} \\\\\n\\leanentry{firstmomentA}{Theorem~\\ref{thm:firstmoment} and its consequence: the weighted sum is at most $\\exp(-\\delta n+3\\ln n+ca)$ for $n\\ge\\max(3,c)$, at most $e^{c/2}n^{3}e^{-\\delta n}$, and tends to $0$ when $\\delta>0$; the trinomial entropy bound from the binomial theorem} \\leannames{firstMoment_pathwidth_le_fin, firstMoment_pathwidth_le_fin', tendsto_firstMoment_pathwidth, choose_mul_choose_le_exp_entropy} \\\\\n\\leanentry{clique-cover}{Appendix~\\ref{app:random-cs}: for independent uniform patterns of given sizes, the probability that the instance's MOSP value is at most $k+1$ (its pathwidth at most $k$) is at most $\\binom nt\\binom{n-t}{t-1}\\prod_j N(s_j)/\\binom n{s_j}$, $N(s)$ the number of $s$-sets missing $A$ or missing $R$} \\leannames{prob_coversOfMospValueLe_le, prob_coversOfPathwidthLe_le, card_coversOfPathwidthLe_le, card_filter_avoid} \\\\\n\\leanentry{coupling}{Appendix~\\ref{app:random-cs}, the coupling: if every pattern of one cover is contained in the corresponding pattern of another, every empty pair of the larger cover's graph is an empty pair of the smaller's, and the pathwidth is monotone} \\leannames{isEmptyPair_coverGraph_of_subset, pathwidth_coverGraph_mono} \\\\\n\\leanentry{blowup}{Theorem~\\ref{thm:blowup}, the blow-up bound: for every instance, $\\mathrm{MOSP}\\le\\omega\\,(\\pw(B)+1)$ with $\\omega$ the largest pattern and $B$ the customer--pattern incidence graph (as a \\texttt{SimpleGraph} on the sum type); a path decomposition of $B$ blown up to one of the MOSP graph} \\leannames{MOSPInstance.mospValue_le_maxPatternSize_mul, MOSPInstance.pathwidth_mospGraph_add_one_le, MOSPInstance.blowUp_width_add_one_le} \\\\\n\\leanentry{forest}{Theorem~\\ref{thm:blowup}, the forest form: if $B$ is acyclic, $\\mathrm{MOSP}\\le\\omega(\\lfloor\\log_3((6(n+m)+3)/5)\\rfloor+1)$; a forest on $N$ vertices has pathwidth at most $\\lfloor\\log_3((6N+3)/5)\\rfloor$, the least $k$ with $N\\le(5\\cdot3^k-3)/2$; a tree on at most $(5\\cdot3^k-3)/2$ vertices has pathwidth at most $k$} \\leannames{MOSPInstance.mospValue_le_of_incidence_isAcyclic, pathwidth_le_treeBoundInv_of_isAcyclic, le_treeBound_treeBoundInv, treeBound_lt_of_lt_treeBoundInv, tree_pathwidth_le_treeBound} \\\\\n\\leanentry{mono}{Appendix~\\ref{app:excess}: the MOSP value is monotone under adding requirements (Yanasse's subinstance monotonicity)} \\leannames{MOSPInstance.mospValue_mono} \\\\\n\\leanentry{layer}{Lemma~\\ref{lem:layer}, the layer lemma: for any level function with adjacent vertices at most one level apart, $\\pw\\le\\max_i(|L_i|+|L_{i+1}|)-1$, in pathwidth and vertex-separation form, and for the distance layers from any vertex of a connected graph} \\leannames{pathwidth_le_layerWidth, vertexSeparation_le_layerWidth, pathwidth_le_layerWidth_dist} \\\\\n\\leanentry{indep}{Lemma~\\ref{lem:layer}, the independent-set bound $\\pw\\le n-\\alpha$, by the bags $(V\\setminus I)\\cup\\{v\\}$} \\leannames{pathwidth_le_card_sub_indepNum} \\\\\n\\leanentry{pendant}{Lemma~\\ref{lem:pendant}, the two-sided pendant bound: a clique $R$ with $q\\ge1$ cliques of size at least $t$ meeting $R$ in one vertex each, at distinct vertices, forces $\\pw\\ge t+\\lceil q/2\\rceil-2$; for any graph, for one decomposition, in the depth-one case $t=q=|R|$, and in MOSP terms (a pattern with $q$ patterns of size at least $t$ hanging off distinct customers forces $\\mathrm{MOSP}\\ge t+\\lceil q/2\\rceil-1$)} \\leannames{pathwidth_ge_of_pendant_cliques, PathDecomposition.pendant_bound, pathwidth_ge_of_clique_pendant_cliques, MOSPInstance.mospValue_ge_of_pendant_patterns} \\\\\n\\leanentry{endpoint}{Lemma~\\ref{lem:endpoint}, the premises as commitment conditions} \\leannames{isDefinite_iff_endpoint, isHereditarilyDefinite_iff_isCommittable} \\\\\n\\leanentry{cexnotcommit}{In Counterexam